>> A2: Briefly describe how you implemented argument parsing.  How do
>> you arrange for the elements of argv[] to be in the right order?
>> How do you avoid overflowing the stack page?

process_execute() copies the command line into a fresh page and
names the new thread after the first word only, so exit messages
show "args-single" and not the whole line.

In start_process() the copy is split with strtok_r() on spaces
(repeated spaces count as one) into a local argv[] array.  argv[0]
is passed to load().  After load() has set up the empty stack page
with esp = PHYS_BASE, push_arguments() builds the stack:

  1. copies the strings from argv[argc-1] down to argv[0], and
     overwrites each argv[i] with the user address it was copied to;
  2. rounds esp down to a multiple of 4;
  3. pushes a NULL pointer for argv[argc];
  4. pushes argv[argc-1] ... argv[0], so argv[0] ends up at the
     lowest address, which is the order main() expects;
  5. pushes argv (the address of argv[0]), argc and a fake return
     address of 0.

Before writing anything, push_arguments() adds up the total size
(string bytes rounded up to a word, plus argc + 4 words) and fails
if it is more than PGSIZE.  More than MAX_ARGS words also fails.  A
failure is treated like a failed load: the process exits with -1
and exec returns -1.

---- RATIONALE ----

>> A3: Why does Pintos implement strtok_r() but not strtok()?

strtok() keeps its position in a hidden static variable, so two
threads tokenizing at the same time would corrupt each other's
state.  strtok_r() keeps the position in a caller-supplied pointer
(save_ptr), which lives on each thread's own stack, so it is safe
in a preemptive kernel.

>> A4: In Pintos, the kernel separates commands into a executable name
>> and arguments.  In Unix-like systems, the shell does this
>> separation.  Identify at least two advantages of the Unix approach.

1. Less code and fewer bugs in the kernel.  Parsing runs in user
   mode, so a parsing bug can only crash the shell, not the OS.

2. The shell can do much richer processing before exec: quoting,
   wildcard expansion, variables, redirection and pipes, without
   the kernel having to know any of those rules.  Different shells
   can use different syntaxes.

3. The shell can check the program exists (search PATH) before
   asking the kernel to create a process.

