    struct file_desc
      {
        int fd;                       /* Number the user sees. */
        struct file *file;            /* The open file. */
        struct list_elem elem;        /* Elem in thread's fd_list. */
      };

        Maps one of a process's fd numbers to an open file.

    struct lock filesys_lock;

        Global lock held around every call into the file system
        (syscalls, load(), and closing files at exit).

>> B2: Describe how file descriptors are associated with open files.
>> Are file descriptors unique within the entire OS or just within a
>> single process?

Each successful open() mallocs a struct file_desc, gives it the
thread's next_fd (then increments it) and pushes it onto the
thread's fd_list.  Other calls look up the fd by walking that list.
close() removes and frees the entry.  Numbers are never reused
inside a process.

File descriptors are unique only within one process: every process
starts counting at 2.  Opening the same file twice, even in the same
process, gives two fds with separate struct file and separate
positions.

---- ALGORITHMS ----

>> B3: Describe your code for reading and writing user data from the
>> kernel.

We check every user address before the kernel touches it, using
pagedir_get_page():

  - is_valid_ptr(p): p is not NULL, is below PHYS_BASE, and is mapped
    in the current page directory.
  - check_buffer(p, size): the buffer must not wrap around, its first
    and last byte must be valid, and one byte in each page in the
    middle must be valid.
  - check_string(s): checks each byte until the terminating '\0'.

The syscall number and every argument are read through
check_buffer(esp or argument address, 4), so a stack pointer or an
argument that sits across a page boundary or past PHYS_BASE is
caught.  If any check fails, sys_exit(-1) kills the process.

Once checked, the kernel reads and writes the user memory directly
through the user virtual address (the process's page directory is
active during the syscall).  read() and write() pass the user
buffer straight to file_read()/file_write(), input_getc() or
putbuf().

>> B4: Suppose a system call causes a full page (4,096 bytes) of data
>> to be copied from user space into the kernel.  What is the least
>> and the greatest possible number of inspections of the page table
>> (e.g. calls to pagedir_get_page()) that might result?  What about
>> for a system call that only copies 2 bytes of data?  Is there room
>> for improvement in these numbers, and how much?

Full page: check_buffer() always makes 3 calls: the first byte, the
last byte, and one in the loop (the page boundary inside the buffer,
or the start again when the buffer is page aligned).  The minimum
needed is 1 when the buffer is page aligned (it is all one page) and
2 when it is not (it spans two pages).

2 bytes: always 2 calls (first and last byte).  The minimum needed
is 1 when both bytes are in the same page and 2 when they straddle
a page boundary.

So there is room to save up to 2 calls in the full-page case and 1
in the 2-byte case, by skipping bytes that fall in a page we already
checked.  The larger improvement would be to drop the checks and
rely on the page fault handler (the get_user()/put_user() approach
in the Pintos manual), which costs 0 page table inspections in the
common, valid case.

>> B5: Briefly describe your implementation of the "wait" system call
>> and how it interacts with process termination.

wait(pid) calls process_wait().  It walks the caller's children
list for a child_status with that tid.  If there is none (not our
child, or already waited for) it returns -1 right away.

Otherwise it does sema_down(&cs->exit_sema).  When the child
terminates in any way (exit(), a kernel kill after a page fault, a
bad pointer, or a failed load), thread_exit() calls process_exit(),
which prints "name: exit(status)", copies exit_status into the
child_status and ups exit_sema.  The parent wakes up, reads the
status, removes the record from its list and drops its reference.
Because the record is gone from the list, a second wait for the same
pid returns -1.

>> B6: Any access to user program memory at a user-specified address
>> can fail due to a bad pointer value.  Such accesses must cause the
>> process to be terminated.  System calls are fraught with such
>> accesses, e.g. a "write" system call requires reading the system
>> call number from the user stack, then each of the call's three
>> arguments, then an arbitrary amount of user memory, and any of
>> these can fail at any point.  This poses a design and
>> error-handling problem: how do you best avoid obscuring the primary
>> function of code in a morass of error-handling?  Furthermore, when
>> an error is detected, how do you ensure that all temporarily
>> allocated resources (locks, buffers, etc.) are freed?  In a few
>> paragraphs, describe the strategy or strategies you adopted for
>> managing these issues.  Give an example.

All checking lives in a few small helpers (get_syscall_arg,
check_buffer, check_string) that do not return on failure: they call
sys_exit(-1).  So a syscall branch reads like the plain happy path,
with a single check line before any user memory is used.

The rule that keeps cleanup simple: validate everything first, before
taking any lock or allocating anything.  Then a failed check can
never leave a lock held or a buffer allocated.  Everything a process
owns for longer (open files, its executable, child records, its page
directory) is released in one place, process_exit(), which runs for
every kind of exit, normal or not.

Example, write(fd, buffer, size):

    int fd = get_syscall_arg (f, 0);          may exit(-1)
    const void *buffer = get_syscall_arg (f, 1);
    unsigned size = get_syscall_arg (f, 2);
    check_buffer (buffer, size);              may exit(-1)
    ... only now: lock_acquire (&filesys_lock);
                  file_write (...);
                  lock_release (&filesys_lock);

If buffer points into kernel memory, check_buffer() exits before
filesys_lock is acquired.  process_exit() then closes the process's
files (taking filesys_lock itself), and nothing is leaked.

If a user program itself touches a bad address, the page fault
handler's kill() calls thread_exit(); exit_status is still its
default of -1, so the process prints "exit(-1)" and is cleaned up by
the same process_exit() path.

---- SYNCHRONIZATION ----

>> B7: The "exec" system call returns -1 if loading the new executable
>> fails, so it cannot return before the new executable has completed
>> loading.  How does your code ensure this?  How is the load
>> success/failure status passed back to the thread that calls "exec"?

process_execute() mallocs the child_status (load_sema at 0) and
passes it to the new thread through struct start_info.  After
thread_create() it does sema_down(&cs->load_sema).

The child, in start_process(), parses the arguments, calls load() and
push_arguments(), stores the result in cs->load_success, and only
then does sema_up(&cs->load_sema).  The parent wakes up and reads
load_success.  If it is false, the parent drops its reference and
returns TID_ERROR (-1); otherwise it adds the record to its children
list and returns the tid.

This also keeps start_info safe: it is on the parent's stack, and the
parent cannot return until the child has finished using it.

>> B8: Consider parent process P with child process C.  How do you
>> ensure proper synchronization and avoid race conditions when P
>> calls wait(C) before C exits?  After C exits?  How do you ensure
>> that all resources are freed in each case?  How about when P
>> terminates without waiting, before C exits?  After C exits?  Are
>> there any special cases?

The shared child_status starts with ref_cnt = 2 (one for P, one for
C).  Whoever lets go of it decrements ref_cnt with interrupts off,
and whoever brings it to 0 frees it.  So it is freed exactly once,
by whichever side finishes last.

- P waits before C exits: P blocks on exit_sema.  C stores its
  status, ups exit_sema and drops its reference (2 -> 1).  P wakes,
  reads the status, drops its reference (1 -> 0) and frees it.

- P waits after C exits: C already stored the status and upped the
  semaphore, so P's sema_down returns at once with the right status.
  C's reference is already gone, so P's release frees the record.

- P exits before C, without waiting: process_exit() in P drops P's
  reference on every record in its children list (2 -> 1).  When C
  exits later it writes to the still-valid record and frees it
  (1 -> 0).  Nobody waits on the semaphore, which is fine.

- P exits after C, without waiting: C already dropped to 1; P's
  process_exit() takes it to 0 and frees it.

Special cases: if C fails to load, it exits (dropping its reference)
and P, in exec, drops the other one, so the record is freed and
never enters P's list.  The initial "main" kernel thread waits on
the first process the same way, it simply has no child_status of
its own.  C's own open files and executable are always freed by C's
process_exit(), independent of P.

---- RATIONALE ----

>> B9: Why did you choose to implement access to user memory from the
>> kernel in the way that you did?

Checking with pagedir_get_page() before use is the simpler of the
two methods in the manual.  It keeps every check in plain C next to
the syscall that needs it, needs no changes to the page fault
handler, and never lets the kernel fault while holding a lock, which
makes the cleanup rules in B6 easy.  The cost is a few page table
lookups per call (B4), which is small for the sizes the tests use.

>> B10: What advantages or disadvantages can you see to your design
>> for file descriptors?

Advantages: the list grows with the number of open files, so there is
no fixed limit, and struct thread only grows by a list and an int,
which keeps the kernel stack safe.  Every fd is freed on close or at
exit, so nothing leaks.  fds are per process, so one process cannot
use another's files.

Disadvantages: finding an fd is a linear walk of the list, which is
slow for a process with many open files (an array or hash would be
O(1)).  fd numbers are never reused, so a process that opens and
closes files forever could eventually overflow next_fd.

>> B11: The default tid_t to pid_t mapping is the identity mapping.
>> If you changed it, what advantages are there to your approach?

We kept the identity mapping.  Each user process is exactly one
thread, so a separate pid would add bookkeeping with no benefit.

